{"causes": ["The ongoing Second Punic War necessitated the election of experienced military commanders like Centumalus Maximus to defend the Roman Republic.", "Hannibal Barca's devastating campaigns in Italy after 218 BCE created a persistent military threat that required constant Roman counter-mobilization.", "The Roman political system, particularly the consulship, propelled individuals like Fulvius Centumalus into positions of military and civil authority during crises."], "effects": ["The defeat and death of Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus at Herdonia marked one of Hannibal's last major battlefield victories in Italy.", "His loss underscored the continued tactical brilliance of Hannibal and the severe challenges faced by Roman commanders even late in the war.", "The Roman Senate's unwavering commitment to replace lost armies demonstrated the Republic's immense resilience and resourcefulness, a key factor in its eventual triumph."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "second-punic-war", "sourceName": "Second Punic War", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "gnaeus-fulvius-centumalus-maximus", "targetName": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus", "context": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus served as a consul and proconsul for Rome during the Second Punic War (218–201 BCE)."}, {"sourceSlug": "gnaeus-fulvius-centumalus-maximus", "sourceName": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "publius-sulpicius-galba-maximus", "targetName": "Publius Sulpicius Galba Maximus", "context": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus served as co-consul with Publius Sulpicius Galba Maximus in 211 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "battle-of-herdonia-209-bce", "sourceName": "Battle of Herdonia (209 BCE)", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "gnaeus-fulvius-centumalus-maximus", "targetName": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus", "context": "The Battle of Herdonia in 209 BCE resulted in the death of Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus."}, {"sourceSlug": "gnaeus-fulvius-centumalus-maximus", "sourceName": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "defense-of-rome-211-bce", "targetName": "Defense of Rome (211 BCE)", "context": "As consul, Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus helped defend Rome against Hannibal's advance in 211 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "herdonia-italy", "sourceName": "Herdonia, Italy", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "gnaeus-fulvius-centumalus-maximus", "targetName": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus", "context": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus met his death in battle near Herdonia, Apulia, in 209 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Capital defended"}, {"name": "Herdonia, Italy", "role": "Site of defeat and death"}, {"name": "Apulia, Italy", "role": "Proconsular command region"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "gnaeus-fulvius-centumalus-maximus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q974512", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnaeus_Fulvius_Centumalus_Maximus"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus's career and tragic death highlight the immense challenges faced by Roman leadership during the Second Punic War, underscoring both Hannibal's enduring tactical genius and Rome's ultimate resilience in the face of devastating losses.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-20T11:37:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35507144199", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus was a consul of the Roman Republic in 211 BC. As consul, Fulvius defended Rome against Hannibal with his colleague Publius Sulpicius Galba Maximus during the Second Punic War.", "newValue": "Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus, a prominent Roman consul, played a critical, albeit ultimately tragic, role in the desperate struggle against Hannibal Barca during the Second Punic War. Elected consul in 211 BCE, his tenure marked a pivotal moment when Rome faced existential threats following the Battle of Cannae, yet also demonstrated the Republic's resilience in defending its heartland. His military career, culminating in his command in Apulia, underscores the immense pressure and high stakes for Roman leadership during this protracted conflict against Carthage.

As consul in 211 BCE, Centumalus Maximus, alongside his colleague Publius Sulpicius Galba Maximus, was instrumental in the defense of Rome itself. While Hannibal made his famous march on Rome (known as the "Hannibal ad portas" episode), Centumalus's consular army, along with other forces, successfully deterred a direct assault on the capital. Following his consulship, he continued to serve as proconsul, commanding forces in Apulia. In 209 BCE, he engaged Hannibal's main army near Herdonia. Despite initial successes in harassing Carthaginian foraging parties, Centumalus's forces were decisively routed by Hannibal in a sudden, devastating ambush, resulting in the annihilation of his army and his own death.

The catastrophic defeat and death of Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus Maximus at Herdonia in 209 BCE represented one of the last major Carthaginian victories against a Roman field army in Italy during the Second Punic War. This loss, coming after years of attrition, underscored the enduring tactical genius of Hannibal and the formidable challenge he continued to pose, even as Roman strategic advantages began to mount. However, the Roman Senate's swift and unwavering response—dispatching new legions and commanders—demonstrated the Republic's unparalleled capacity for resilience and resource mobilization, a trait that ultimately secured its victory in the war."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-20T11:37:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35507144199", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "244 BCE, Ancient Rome"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "244 BCE, Ancient Rome"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Second Punic War necessitated the election of experienced military commanders like Centumalus Maximus to defend the Roman Republic.", "Hannibal Barca's devastating campaigns in Italy after 218 BCE created a persistent military threat that required constant Roman counter-mobilization.", "The Roman political system, particularly the consulship, propelled individuals like Fulvius Centumalus into positions of military and civil authority during crises."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-20T11:37:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35507144199", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "209 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "209 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The defeat and death of Gnaeus Ful